  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 PM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nRadar imagery showing light returns across more of East Idaho \nthan expected, but surface observations indicating amounts so \nfar have generally been flurries or trace amounts. Higher \nreturns still on track to impact the central mountains \nespecially overnight with second surge of showers into the \nregion. Accumulations remain light, with up to 3\" of \naccumulation through Monday across the higher elevations of the \nSawtooths and ranges surrounding the Wood River region. \nElsewhere, flurries are the general expectation, though a few \nlocations could see a light dusting even down to valley floors \nespecially overnight. This shortwave feature shifts through the \nregion during the day Monday, allowing a ridge of high pressure \nto cross the region through Tuesday for dry and continued chilly\nconditions. \n\nModels have shifted on the details regarding the next set of\nsystems arriving for the Thanksgiving holiday. The GFS is still\nindicating an open semi-split trough that loses its moisture as\nit reaches Idaho Wednesday, and the ECMWF is now following the\nsame idea. Both models introduce zonal flow for Thanksgiving\nday, which transitions into a moist northwesterly flow by\nFriday. Ensemble means support a continued chance for light\nmountain snow each period Thanksgiving day through the weekend.\nA mix of valley rain/snow or snow will be possible at times as\nwell. At this time precipitation amounts do not look impactful,\nbut the mountain snow could produce travel inconveniences at\ntimes, especially if the scenarios lean toward higher-end\nprobabilities for any particular day.",
